--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0, "Index is not within the range of the array");

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

            Debug.Assert(Old_index != Old_arrListCount, "index passed in is not equal to the old array size");
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1, "New array size is not one more than the old array size");
            Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet, "New array does not contain the retrieved value");

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);
        }
    }
}
```
This code includes `Debug.Assert` statements in the specified places. The `Debug.Assert` statements are used to verify assumptions made in the code. The first one checks that the index we're getting is within the bounds of the list. The second one checks if the new list size is one more than the old list size after the new element was added. The third one ensures the new list contains the value that was retrieved. In production code, these assertions could trigger errors or exceptions when their conditions are not met, allowing developers to quickly detect and fix bugs.
--------------------Here are the assert statements corresponding to the comments starting with "@@@".

```cs
// Asserts that the index passed in is not equal to the old array size
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != index);

```
In the specified C# method, the condition to check is if the "index" being passed in is not equal to the old size of the array list "arrList". 

So, the Debug.Assert statement is written as follows:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != index);
```
This statement will evaluate the condition and if it is true, the execution will proceed normally. If it is false, which means the condition was not met, a debug assertion dialog box will be shown, giving the user the option to break into the debugger or proceed without debugging (which will simply terminate the program).
--------------------Here are the assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" in the provided C# code:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != index); 
Debug.Assert((Old_arrListCount - 1) != index); 
```

These are the asserts that check whether the index passed in is not equal to the old array size. The first assert checks whether the index is different than the count of the array before the operation. The second assert checks whether index minus one is different from the count of the array. If any of these asserts fail, it means the index is out of range or the array is not updated correctly after the operation.
--------------------Based on the given C# code, I am going to create the following assert statements:

```cs
            Debug.Assert(index != Old_arrListCount, "index passed in is not equal to the old array size");
```

The assertion line `Debug.Assert(index != Old_arrListCount, "index passed in is not equal to the old array size");` asserts that the value of `index` is not equal to the old array size. It's a condition which checks if the 'index' parameter passed in the method is not equal to the old array list count. The string argument "index passed in is not equal to the old array size" is a message that will be displayed in the debug output window if the assertion fails.
